---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Nordstrom eGifting — Rob Pitt"
  description="Designed the first eGifting flow with Nordstrom. 72 hours to concept, $100k in the first week."
>
  <article class="case-study">
    <header class="case-study__header">
      <div class="container">
        <p class="case-study__meta">CashStar × Nordstrom · 2015 · Portland, ME</p>
        <h1 class="case-study__title">The first eGifting flow, from zero to $100k in a week.</h1>
        <p class="case-study__lede">
          Retailers were bleeding on returns. I identified an opportunity: let recipients choose size or color <em>before</em> the gift ships. Then I had 72 hours to concept it, sell it to Nordstrom's leadership, and design it into existence.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="case-study__section case-study__section--wide">
      <figure class="figure">
        <img src="/case-studies/egifting/egifting-LIVE.png" alt="Nordstrom eGifting on launch day" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>Live on Nordstrom.com — December 14, 2015.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section class="case-study__section">
      <div class="container narrow">
        <p class="case-study__label">The problem</p>
        <p>
          Returns eat retailer margins. Gift returns are worse — the recipient didn't pick it, so they don't want it, so back it goes into a warehouse that costs money to run. If the recipient could pick size, color, or swap for store credit <em>before</em> fulfillment, nobody ships nothing back.
        </p>
        <p>
          The concept was simple. Selling it to a Fortune 500 department store in 72 hours was not.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-study__section">
      <div class="container narrow">
        <p class="case-study__label">My role</p>
        <p>As Lead Designer:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Concept and hi-fi mockups within 72 hours</li>
          <li>Competitor analysis (LOOP Commerce, Jifiti) and usability testing</li>
          <li>Direct work with Nordstrom's team on brand and integration</li>
          <li>Both the purchase experience and the redemption experience</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-study__section case-study__section--wide">
      <figure class="figure figure--split">
        <div>
          <img src="/case-studies/egifting/Step2.png" alt="Recipient wireframe — purchase" loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption>Recipient purchase wireframe.</figcaption>
        </div>
        <div>
          <img src="/case-studies/egifting/Step3.png" alt="Recipient wireframe — checkout" loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption>Recipient checkout wireframe.</figcaption>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section class="case-study__section">
      <div class="container narrow">
        <p class="case-study__label">Rapid research and concepting</p>
        <p>
          I documented existing processes, sketched initial ideas in Balsamiq, and — without time for the usual round of PM validation — moved directly into Sketch to build flows and visuals. Agile out of necessity, not aesthetics.
        </p>

        <p class="case-study__label">Executive presentation and iteration</p>
        <p>
          The first comps went straight to our CEO and VP for a presentation to Nordstrom. The three-hour time zone difference gave me a small buffer for feedback. When change requests came back for Monday's presentation, my weekend was spoken for. I built a clickable prototype to demonstrate the product-based gifting flow. Monday's presentation secured the project.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-study__section case-study__section--wide">
      <figure class="figure figure--split">
        <div>
          <img src="/case-studies/egifting/whiteboard1.png" alt="Whiteboarding session 1" loading="lazy" />
        </div>
        <div>
          <img src="/case-studies/egifting/whiteboard2.png" alt="Whiteboarding session 2" loading="lazy" />
        </div>
      </figure>
      <figure class="figure">
        <img src="/case-studies/egifting/whiteboard3.png" alt="Whiteboarding session 3" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>Two days on-site whiteboarding with Nordstrom's team.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section class="case-study__section">
      <div class="container narrow">
        <p class="case-study__label">Testing and validation</p>
        <p>Remote unmoderated usability testing with the scenario:</p>
        <blockquote>
          "Your best friend's birthday is coming up soon, and you wanted to send her something special. She just moved across the country and you don't have time to mail a gift. You decided to try sending an eGift of something you think she'll love instead."
        </blockquote>
        <p>Critical insights that changed the design:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Users needed more explanation of this new concept</li>
          <li>The add-on tax created confusion about who pays</li>
          <li>Preview functionality for the recipient experience was essential</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-study__section case-study__section--wide">
      <figure class="figure figure--split">
        <div>
          <img src="/case-studies/egifting/live-step1.png" alt="Live Nordstrom eGifting step 1" loading="lazy" />
        </div>
        <div>
          <img src="/case-studies/egifting/live-step3.png" alt="Live Nordstrom eGifting step 3" loading="lazy" />
        </div>
      </figure>
      <figure class="figure">
        <img src="/case-studies/egifting/live-step4.png" alt="Live Nordstrom eGifting step 4" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>The flow that shipped.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section class="case-study__section">
      <div class="container narrow">
        <p class="case-study__label">The result</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>$100,000+ in the first week.</strong></li>
          <li>Only solution in the industry handling eGifting without direct inventory management.</li>
          <li>Configurable platform — adaptable to other retail partners without another redesign.</li>
          <li>Successfully implemented with Nordstrom's full-price product line.</li>
        </ul>

        <blockquote>
          "Fantastic news. Please make sure Rob knows how much we appreciate his kick ass, late night and weekend work on the Nordstrom mock ups. They were great and I am sure invaluable in allowing Nordstrom to visualize the possibilities."
          <cite>— Matt K., VP Strategic Business Development</cite>
        </blockquote>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-study__section">
      <div class="container narrow">
        <p class="case-study__label">What I actually did</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Delivered high-quality design under extreme time pressure — the 72-hour turnaround secured a major retail partner.</li>
          <li>Insisted on user testing despite the timeline, which validated our approach against Nordstrom's independent findings.</li>
          <li>Bridged executive team, product management, and Nordstrom's design team into one aligned vision.</li>
          <li>Designed a configurable solution that scaled beyond Nordstrom without another redesign.</li>
          <li>Advocated for mobile despite it not being in the initial requirements — turned out we needed it.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <footer class="case-study__footer">
      <div class="container narrow">
        <a href="/#shipped" class="case-study__back">← back to all work</a>
      </div>
    </footer>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  @import "../../styles/case-study.css";
</style>
